1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:16,600
Seven modules taught you seven capabilities in isolation. This chapter is about what happens
when you assemble them into one coherent product — because that's a separate skill, and it's
the one interviews probe.

2
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:22,600
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:32,600
It started as one sentence: a cinema ticket-booking app where you browse films, pick a
schedule, choose seats, see the price and confirm a reservation.

4
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:45,000
That became a requirements list — deliberately unglamorous. Movie list. Movie details.
Showtimes. Seat selection. Booking summary. Confirmation. A bookings history. Cancellation.
Validation alerts. A list a non-developer could have written.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:01:01,800
And one thing was missing, which an early review caught: there was no profile screen. Adding
accounts turned out to unlock roughly a third of everything else — bookings could be owned,
tickets shared, reviews gated on whether you held a ticket.

6
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:11,000
That's the strongest argument for writing requirements down where somebody else can read and
question them, rather than holding them in your head.

7
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,400
The requirements became this flowchart. Rectangles are processes, diamonds are decisions.

8
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:32,200
Trace the happy path first — start, browse, select, confirm, done. Now look at everything
else. Not signed in, routing off to account creation. No seat selected, routing to a
validation alert. Cancel, which releases the seats and cancels the pending reminder.

9
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:46,200
Most of real app design is the handling of things going wrong. Those branches are where bugs
breed, and drawing them before any code existed is why they were handled deliberately rather
than discovered later.

10
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:59,000
Then a wireframe — rough sketches deciding only what goes where, not what it looks like.
Chasing pixel-perfect visuals at that stage wastes effort on decisions that change once real
content arrives.

11
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:19,800
Two things changed between plan and build, and both are healthy. The 'how to use' tab was
cut — it existed only to fill a fourth tab, and Settings made it redundant. And a payment
step was scoped out on purpose: better to ship no payment than a fake one that misleads.

12
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:27,400
Here's the result. Five taps from browsing a film to a confirmed, locally-persisted ticket
with its own booking ID.

13
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:39,400
And every step is a module doing real work. The detail screen is Module two. The data is
Modules four and five. The structure holding it together is Module six.

14
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:50,200
One design detail deserves its own moment, because it's the reuse the architecture module
was building toward. Movies book by seat. Concerts and seminars book by quantity.

15
00:02:50,200 --> 00:03:05,000
Rather than two separate booking systems, there's a single reservation core with two
different front-ends. That's exactly the reuse Clean Architecture and protocol-based
injection are supposed to make possible — and here it is, on screen, working.

16
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Let's recap the key points.

17
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,100
Chapter nine proves it all works — and shows why the architecture from Module six was worth
the effort.
